\subsection{Multi-Seed Online Dual Control under Reward Rescale}
\label{app:dual_details}

Section~\ref{sec:dual_multiseed} summarizes this study. Proposition~\ref{prop:pi1} carries an offline count-budget bracket across a known reward-and-cost rescaling, but not across an unannounced one, so a deployed controller must then re-adapt online. The setting is Diagnosis with target $B{=}8$, learning rate $\eta_0{=}0.05$, decay $\delta{=}0.02$, and a $\times 10$ reward-and-cost rescale at episode~$200$ of a $400$-episode run, with $10$ independent controller seeds for each of two variants. The decay-only variant runs the update of Equation~\ref{eq:dual_update} with its step decaying from the first episode and never intervenes. The reset-on-shift variant adds the change-detection heuristic of Section~\ref{sec:pi5}. It watches a rolling window of per-episode usage (window~$20$), and once the step has decayed below half its initial value it declares a shift when the window mean departs from $B$ by more than the larger of $k$ standard errors of the window mean ($k{=}3$) and an absolute floor of $0.5$ observations. A declared shift restarts the step-size clock of Section~\ref{sec:pi5}, so the step is large again.

Re-adaptation time is measured on a rolling mean of usage that, at each post-rescale episode, averages per-episode usage over the previous $20$ episodes and is computed from post-rescale episodes only. Re-adaptation time is the number of episodes after the rescale until that rolling mean stays within $\pm 1$ of $B$ for $20$ consecutive episodes. Restricting the window to post-rescale episodes matters. A window allowed to straddle the rescale point reports a spuriously fast recovery by mixing in already-converged pre-rescale usage (\texttt{experiments/\allowbreak run\_\allowbreak price\_\allowbreak of\_\allowbreak information.py:\allowbreak readaptation\_\allowbreak episodes}).

Resetting the step-size clock re-adapts roughly $3\times$ faster than decay alone. The comparison of record uses one estimand for every seed, the restricted recovery time $\min(T, 180)$, where $T$ is the post-rescale-only recovery time and $180$ is the last index at which the $20$-episode hold can still be checked inside the $200$ post-rescale episodes, with a seed that never completes the hold scored at $180$. On that estimand decay-only averages $157.3$ episodes and reset-on-shift $51.2$, a ratio of $3.07$. Because the two variants share their ten controller seeds, the paired difference is $106.1$ episodes with a Student-$t$ $95\%$ interval of $[96.8, 115.4]$, and every one of the ten paired differences is positive, the smallest being $89$ (\texttt{results\_\allowbreak price\_\allowbreak dual\_\allowbreak multiseed\_\allowbreak metrics.csv}, column \texttt{readapt\_\allowbreak restricted}, and the summary JSON's \texttt{dual\_\allowbreak ms\_\allowbreak readapt\_\allowbreak restricted} block). For descriptive context only, decay-only recovery averages $154.8$ episodes, $95\%$ CI $[142.7, 166.9]$, over the $9/10$ seeds that recover inside the window. Two of those nine recover only at episode $180$, and the tenth seed's rolling mean lies within tolerance over the final episodes without having held there for $20$ consecutive episodes, so the decay-only sample is right-censored at $180$ and its restricted mean is a lower bound on recovery time for these ten runs, not a population inference. Reset-on-shift recovers on $10/10$ seeds, each triggering exactly one step-size reset, so its restricted and conditional means coincide ($51.2$, CI $[46.1, 56.3]$). Post-rescale steady-state $|U-B|$, the absolute gap between the budget and mean usage over a fixed block of episodes at the end of the run, is $0.06$ ($[0.024,0.096]$) under reset versus $0.39$ ($[0.118,0.666]$) under decay-only. Pre-rescale steady-state error over the block immediately before the rescale is $0.10$ ($[0.051,0.149]$) for both variants, which diverge only after the rescale, well inside the $\pm 1$ recovery tolerance. Every CI here other than the paired interval is a normal-approximation CI over the controller seeds that enter it, and the separate intervals of the two conditional means are not an interval for their ratio. The battery runs under per-episode seeding, which covers the environment constructed at the rescale. Figure~\ref{fig:dualmultiseed} shows the median trajectory with interquartile band for both variants.

Per seed, reset-on-shift reaches a tighter post-rescale steady state than decay-only on five of the ten seeds, ties on four, and is looser on one. The mid-run rescale makes the environment nonstationary and the reset-on-shift variant restarts its schedule, so neither variant is covered by Proposition~\ref{prop:pi5}'s stationary guarantee, and what is measured here is nonstationary re-adaptation. The mechanism is the residual step size that the discussion after Proposition~\ref{prop:pi5} identifies. At the rescale the decayed step is still large enough to move the controlled weight $w_t$, which is why decay-only recovers at all, but only slowly. Reset-on-shift restarts the schedule as soon as the detector declares the shift, and since the reset is the only thing that distinguishes the two variants, that restored step is where its speed advantage comes from.

