Section~\ref{sec:sarsop} compares EFE with SARSOP \citep{kurniawati2008} on the three core environments ($5$ seeds $\times$ $500$ episodes). The generic Cassandra \texttt{.pomdp} export and shared in-simulator runner are described in Appendix~\ref{app:sarsop_build}. At $\gamma{=}0.999$, the solver closes the initial-belief upper/lower value-bound gap below $10^{-3}$ in under one second per environment. Its alpha-vector policy selects the action attached to the state-value vector with largest inner product with the belief.

Tiger's SARSOP and EFE actions coincide episode by episode. Diagnosis and Bandit show no significant reward difference at this sample size and differ by fewer than $0.1$ observations (Table~\ref{tab:sarsop}). The equivalence tests below assess a stronger claim than non-rejection.

Fixed-margin two one-sided tests (TOST) \citep{schuirmann1987} assess whether the reward difference lies strictly inside $\pm1.0$ on Tiger and Diagnosis or $\pm0.5$ on Bandit, the cost of one sensing action. Rejecting both one-sided nulls supports equivalence within that operational margin. The five-seed margins were chosen after inspecting an earlier comparison on the same canonical seeds, so this test is retrospective. The fifteen additional seeds in the $n{=}20$ check below were fixed after the margin and before that study ran, providing the prospective part of the evidence. The margin is domain-specific, not a universal equivalence standard.

Each one-sided test is an unpaired Welch test on per-seed means ($n{=}5$, $500$ episodes per seed). Sharing seed labels does not make trajectories independent: Tiger's are identical, whereas Diagnosis and Bandit consume random draws in different orders. The resulting covariance is not known from the design alone. Treating the samples as independent is conservative only when that covariance is positive, since $\mathrm{Var}(X-Y)=\mathrm{Var}(X)+\mathrm{Var}(Y)-2\mathrm{Cov}(X,Y)$. The paired sensitivity analysis below checks the effect on inference using per-seed differences. Episode matching is addressed separately.

All three environments reject both one-sided nulls at $\alpha{=}0.05$, where the reported TOST $p$-value, written $p_{\mathrm{TOST}}$ below, is the larger of the two one-sided $p$-values (Tiger $p{=}0.0010$, Diagnosis $p{=}0.0458$, Bandit $p{=}0.0130$). The same fact viewed as an interval is that the $90\%$ CI of each mean difference lies entirely inside the margin (Tiger $[-0.41,+0.41]$, Diagnosis $[-0.51,+0.98]$, Bandit $[-0.35,+0.31]$). The interval is $90\%$ because two one-sided tests at level $\alpha$ together correspond to a two-sided interval at confidence $1 - 2\alpha$. The three tests survive Holm--Bonferroni correction as a family. EFE at the canonical weight is therefore statistically equivalent to the SARSOP reference within the fixed margin on this suite (\texttt{results\_\allowbreak tost\_\allowbreak sarsop.csv}).

{\emergencystretch=3em The five-seed sample is small. We reran the identical procedure at $n{=}20$ seeds, fixed before running rather than added post hoc, reusing the same SARSOP policies (\texttt{results\_\allowbreak tost\_\allowbreak sarsop\_\allowbreak n20\_\allowbreak robustness.csv}, per-seed means in \texttt{results\_\allowbreak tost\_\allowbreak sarsop\_\allowbreak n20\_\allowbreak robustness\_\allowbreak per\_\allowbreak seed.csv}). All three environments remain equivalent, and $p_{\mathrm{TOST}}$ tightens by roughly six, five, and eight orders of magnitude (Tiger $4.5{\times}10^{-10}$, Diagnosis $6.8{\times}10^{-7}$, Bandit $6.0{\times}10^{-11}$). The intervals narrow accordingly, with Tiger's $90\%$ CI shrinking from $[-0.41,+0.41]$ to $[-0.21,+0.21]$. The paired sensitivity check promised above runs the same two one-sided tests on the five per-seed differences (\texttt{results\_\allowbreak tost\_\allowbreak sarsop.csv}, columns \texttt{p\_\allowbreak tost\_\allowbreak paired} and companions, per-seed means in \texttt{results\_\allowbreak tost\_\allowbreak sarsop\_\allowbreak per\_\allowbreak seed.csv}). On Tiger the differences are identically zero, so the paired test is degenerate and equivalence holds trivially. On Diagnosis the paired $p_{\mathrm{TOST}}$ is $0.016$ against the unpaired $0.046$, and on Bandit it is $3.6{\times}10^{-5}$ against $0.013$, with the paired standard errors of the difference ($0.24$ and $0.03$) well below the unpaired ones ($0.38$ and $0.18$). The shared-seed covariance is therefore positive on both environments, so the unpaired analysis reported above (\texttt{results\_\allowbreak tost\_\allowbreak sarsop.csv}) is the conservative one and the equivalence conclusion does not depend on the choice.\par}

Seed-paired differences are still not episode-matched, because the two policies consume each seed's random stream differently. A predeclared episode-matched check removes that limitation (\texttt{run\_\allowbreak tost\_\allowbreak sarsop.py --episode-seeding}, \texttt{results\_\allowbreak tost\_\allowbreak sarsop\_\allowbreak episode\_\allowbreak paired.csv} and its per-seed file). It resets episode $e$ of seed $s$ at $10^4 s + e$, the budget-frontier convention, so both policies face the same hidden states and the same observation draws for as long as their actions agree, and it uses the same twenty seeds, $500$ episodes per seed, and margins as the $n{=}20$ study. Equivalence holds on all three environments. Tiger's policies act identically. Diagnosis gives a paired difference of $+0.11$ with SE $0.09$ and paired $p_{\mathrm{TOST}}=3.2{\times}10^{-9}$, and Bandit gives $+0.04$ with SE $0.03$ and $p_{\mathrm{TOST}}=4.6{\times}10^{-12}$. The unpaired versions on the same runs give $3.8{\times}10^{-5}$ and $1.8{\times}10^{-8}$.

We do not report a usage-matched comparison with SARSOP, because on Tiger it is ill-posed. SARSOP's usage of $4.32$ sits just below the Planning+IG usage curve's floor of $4.37$ (within one seed-level SE, Table~\ref{tab:w-atlas}), so the offline usage curve has no crossing at that target, and any higher target falls in its single wide step $w \in (19.3, 43.9]$. The usage-matched row in \texttt{results\_\allowbreak sarsop\_\allowbreak baseline.json}, labeled Planning+IG ($w{=}0$), is the resulting low-end grid fallback.

