ut value about $2.8$ such SEs from the twenty-seed estimate, unusual but within the range of seed-set variation. A wrong diagnosis costs $60$ relative to a correct one, so a few errors can move a five-seed mean, and the replication, in which the $0.25$ row moves the opposite way, is consistent with seed-set variation as the source of the discrepancy. That one Diagnosis pair had been evaluated on these seeds before the replication was specified, so the seeds are not blind for it. The plug-in intervals therefore support the aggregate shortfall count, Bandit's rows, and Tiger's largest-budget row, not the identity of the Diagnosis budgets that fall short.

The cap reference is flat at the unpenalized reward wherever $B$ exceeds that policy's usage, which holds at seven of the eleven distinct budgets, so its shortfalls there include the cost of spending $B$. A predeclared target-matched reference removes that component (\texttt{experiments/\allowbreak run\_\allowbreak frontier\_\allowbreak target\_\allowbreak reference.py}, \texttt{results\_\allowbreak cpomdp\_\allowbreak frontier\_\allowbreak subsidy.csv}, \texttt{results\_\allowbreak budget\_\allowbreak frontier\_\allowbreak target\_\allowbreak reference.csv}). SARSOP is solved at eleven subsidized penalties $\lambda=-c\,s$, with $c$ the observation cost and $s$ from $0.1$ to $0.98$, so net observation reward stays negative. The larger subsidies raise held-out usage above the unpenalized level, to at most $7.17$ on Tiger, $16.58$ on Diagnosis, and $11.59$ on Bandit, while the smaller ones leave it unchanged or slightly lower. Every sampled policy is evaluated on the held-out and fresh streams, after the committed policies reproduce their held-out rewards. At each budget the reference is the reward-maximizing mixture of sampled points whose held-out usage equals $B$, fitted on the held-out seeds and frozen for the fresh ones. At the four binding budgets it coincides with the cap reference. At the seven slack budgets its support includes a subsidized policy, and no interval excludes zero on either stream. The held-out target gaps there run from $-0.16\pm0.12$ (Bandit, $B{=}5.51$) to $+0.08\pm0.04$ (Tiger, $B{=}4.72$), and Diagnosis's largest target moves from $-1.10\pm0.22$ against the cap reference to $-0.05\pm0.16$. On the held-out seeds, the Tiger family and reference-support policies earn $10$ minus their usage, so the held-out target gaps equal the mixture's usage shortfall below $B$ and measure no reward efficiency. More generally, the mixture meets $B$ only within its usage error, which a target gap also carries. A post hoc sensitivity analysis, run without new episodes, refits the reference at each mixture's realized held-out usage instead (\texttt{--usage-matched}, \texttt{results\_\allowbreak budget\_\allowbreak frontier\_\allowbreak target\_\allowbreak reference\_\allowbreak usage\_\allowbreak matched.csv}). Every held-out Tiger mean gap becomes zero, Diagnosis still falls short at $B{=}9.53$ on the held-out seeds and at $B{=}7.72$ on the fresh ones, and Bandit's gap-budget interval reaches $+0.001$ on the held-out seeds while still excluding zero on the fresh ones. In the predeclared comparison at $B$, the two held-out shortfalls are Diagnosis at $B{=}9.53$ and Bandit's gap budget. On the fresh seeds they are Diagnosis at $B{=}7.72$ ($-0.56\pm0.24$) and Bandit's gap budget ($-0.50\pm0.11$), so only Bandit's recurs. The reference is still a maximum over a finite set of approximately solved, noisily evaluated policies, so it estimates rather than bounds the best policy meeting each target.

