\paragraph{Spatial scaling.} We scale the grid from $4{\times}4$ to $8{\times}8$ with five agents, Myopic, Planning, InfoGain-Tuned, Planning+IG, and EFE (Figure~\ref{fig:tw_scaling}). The sweep is a separate run from the seven-agent Tileworld battery of Table~\ref{tab:tileworld}, at the smaller episode count stated in the figure's caption, and it omits that battery's untuned Info Gain ($w{=}1$) and Epistemic-only agents, whose full seven-agent tables at $6{\times}6$ and $8{\times}8$ are in Appendix~\ref{app:full_tables}. At $8{\times}8$ ($|S|{=}64$), reward-only Planning collapses to $1.4\% \pm 0.3$pp success while EFE maintains $69.4\% \pm 1.0$pp (seed-level SE). There reward-only Planning takes zero scans and is bit-identical to Myopic on every outcome metric ($0.00$ scans, $1.4\%$ success, $-49.16$ reward for both). A depth-2 lookahead sees at most two scans before it must value a commit at its leaf, and after two binary scans a belief that began uniform over $64$ cells is still spread over many cells, so the commit valued there stays close to chance and its expected improvement over committing at once is smaller than the scans cost. The comparison at this scale is therefore EFE against a non-scanning agent, a joint horizon-and-scale effect at the fixed $H{=}2$ of this sweep, not evidence about reward-only planning at deeper horizons, where the scan would begin to pay for itself.

Across scales, the gap between EFE and Planning is a threshold at $8{\times}8$ rather than a gradual widening. At $4{\times}4$ and $6{\times}6$, EFE's reward and success are within sampling error of reward-only Planning's, within 1 SE at both scales, with Planning nominally slightly ahead on both. No formal test was run for this specific sweep. EFE's advantage opens up only at $8{\times}8$, exactly where Planning's success collapses.

Figure~\ref{fig:tw_scaling}'s two weighted series are not tuned the way the main tables' weighted agents are. Planning+IG at $w{=}100$ achieves $97.8\%$ success but at substantial reward cost from extensive scanning. The scaling sweep fixes $w{=}100$ for both weighted agents, InfoGain-Tuned and Planning+IG, rather than tuning each within its own agent class as the main tables do, so the figure is a fixed-weight comparison, and its InfoGain-Tuned series keeps that label from the main battery only as a series name.



\paragraph{Observation structure sensitivity.} To test whether EFE's Tileworld result depends on the bit-level partition of scan actions, we replace the deterministic row/column splits with random and overlapping partitions at the $6{\times}6$ scale of Table~\ref{tab:tileworld}. At that scale the advantage is the scan-count saving, so scan counts are the primary readout, with reward and success reported alongside. Under random partitions each scan assigns the cells at random to two equal halves, so the six splits no longer correspond to separate index bits and their answers are no longer guaranteed to combine into a unique cell. Under overlapping partitions each scan draws random weights for the row and column indices, scores every cell by the weighted sum of its two normalized coordinates, and splits the cells at a random threshold, so each scan cuts the grid along a random tilted line and different scans cut off overlapping regions, producing correlated and partially redundant observations (construction in \texttt{rho\_\allowbreak aif/\allowbreak environments/\allowbreak tileworld.py}).

No partition mode separates EFE from Planning on reward or on success, while EFE uses fewer scans than Planning under all three. This battery is a separate run at 200 episodes per seed, so its bitwise figures differ slightly from Table~\ref{tab:tileworld} ($6{\times}6$, $H{=}2$, 5 seeds, \texttt{results\_\allowbreak partition\_\allowbreak sensitivity\_\allowbreak 6x6.csv}, \texttt{experiments/\allowbreak run\_\allowbreak tileworld.py partition}). EFE against Planning, in reward $\pm$ seed-level SE with success in parentheses, is $-21.62 \pm 0.87$ (71.9\%) vs.\ $-21.41 \pm 0.64$ (73.6\%) under the bitwise partition, $-29.31 \pm 0.43$ (56.1\%) vs.\ $-30.32 \pm 0.66$ (55.1\%) under the random partition, and $-47.53 \pm 0.73$ (8.5\%) vs.\ $-47.66 \pm 0.68$ (8.4\%) under the overlapping partition. The largest reward gap is $1.01$ under the random partition, at $1.28$ seed-level SE of the difference (Welch $p{=}0.24$), and the nominal ordering changes direction across modes. On success the two are within sampling error under all three modes. On scans EFE uses fewer than Planning under all three ($14.76$ vs.\ $15.57$ bitwise, $12.97$ vs.\ $13.38$ random, $2.63$ vs.\ $2.70$ overlapping).

The nominal reward gaps here are within sampling error and are not read as signal. What the battery does establish is a large and consistent effect of observation structure on every agent. Both alternative modes cut absolute performance sharply, from roughly $-21$ reward under the bitwise partition to $-30$ under random scans and $-48$ under overlapping ones, because less complementary scans leave less recoverable information for any agent to exploit. We therefore treat the Tileworld result as scoped to the structured partition it was measured on, and we do not claim this experiment establishes EFE's advantage is model-independent.

