\subsection{Structural Inspection}
\label{app:inspection_details}

This subsection extends Section~\ref{sec:inspection} on the synthetic Structural Inspection POMDP specified in Appendix~\ref{app:experimental_protocol}. Each of the $N$ components must be declared nominal or faulty while the agent stands on it, a declaration that cannot be revised, and declarations are interleaved with the moves and tests. Tests do not alter component states, so this is a factored observation POMDP and Proposition~\ref{prop:factored} applies. In Table~\ref{tab:inspection}, Missed is the mean number of faulty components per episode declared nominal, and Tests is the mean number of tests run per episode. As on RockSample, all tree-search agents here share one hand-coded leaf evaluation (\texttt{rho\_\allowbreak aif/\allowbreak agents/\allowbreak inspection\_\allowbreak agents.py}) that greedily completes the episode from the depth limit, so they differ only in the information-gain weight.



Section~\ref{sec:inspection} summarizes Table~\ref{tab:inspection}. The seed-level tests behind that summary are as follows. On $N{=}8$, EFE's $+18.0$pp accuracy gain over Planning has seed-level Welch $p<10^{-10}$. On $N{=}16$, EFE's reward deficit to Planning ($-45.80$ against $-45.42$) is not significant ($p{=}0.57$), so the two reward cells are bolded together. EFE's $+12.6$pp accuracy gain over Planning has $p<10^{-7}$. Planning+IG ($w{=}5$) runs $38.3 \pm 0.05$ tests against EFE's $28.1 \pm 0.09$ (seed-level SE), roughly $36\%$ more (seed-level Welch $p{=}1.7{\times}10^{-11}$).

Inspection's payoffs also allow a heuristic reading of Proposition~\ref{prop:nearopt}, whose thresholds depend on the reward asymmetry ratio $\alpha = |R^-|/R^+$ (not the reward scale that Section~\ref{sec:collapse} also writes as $\alpha$), the observation accuracy, and the cost. Declaring nominal pays $+2$ when correct and $-50$ on a missed fault ($\alpha = 25$ for that decision), while declaring a fault is symmetric ($+5$ when correct, $-5$ on a false alarm, $\alpha = 1$). Reading the two-state thresholds onto this multi-state environment is a heuristic reduction, as with the two-state-reduced rows of Table~\ref{tab:alpha_eta}. On that reading the missed-fault decision places Inspection in the large-asymmetry regime of Part (a), where a heavy wrong-commit penalty drives the lower threshold $w^*_{\mathrm{thresh}}$ downward and $w{=}1$ clears the interval's lower endpoint once that threshold is below zero. We do not evaluate the over-observation threshold of Part (b) for Inspection, so this places Inspection in the regime where Part (a) stops binding, not $w{=}1$ inside the full interval.

